#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Input {#input}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Both `SamplerV2` and `EstimatorV2` take one or more *primitive unified "
"blocs* (PUBs) as the input. Each PUB is a tuple that contains **one** "
"circuit and the data broadcasted to that circuit, which can be multiple "
"observables and parameters.  Each PUB returns a result.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Sampler V2 PUB format: (`<circuit>`, `<parameter values>`, `<shots>`), where"
" `<parameter values>`and `<shots>` are optional.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"* Estimator V2 PUB format: (`<circuit>`, `<observables>`, `<parameter values>`, `<precision>`), where `<parameter values>`and `<precision>` are optional.\n"
"    Numpy [broadcasting rules](https://numpy.org/doc/stable/user/basics.broadcasting.html) are used when combining observables and parameter values.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Additionally, the following changes have been made:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Estimator V2 has gained a `precision` argument in the `run()` method that "
"specifies the targeted precision of the expectation value estimates.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Sampler V2 has the `shots` argument in its `run()` method.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title #####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Examples {#examples}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Estimator V2 example that uses precision in `run()`:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Sampler V2 example that uses shots in `run()`:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Output {#output}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The output is now in the "
"[`PubResult`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.primitives.PubResult)"
" format. A `PubResult` is the data and metadata resulting from a single "
"PUB’s execution.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Estimator V2 continues to return expectation values.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The `data` portion of a Estimator V2 PubResult contains both expectation "
"values and standard errors (`stds`). V1 returned variance in metadata.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"* Sampler V2 returns per-shot measurements in the form of **bitstrings**,\n"
"   instead of the quasi-probability distributions from the V1\n"
"   interface. The bitstrings show the measurement outcomes, preserving the shot\n"
"   order in which they were measured.\n"
"* Sampler V2 has convenience methods like `get_counts()` to help with migration.\n"
"* The Sampler V2 result objects organize data in terms of their **input circuits' classical register names**, for\n"
"   compatibility with dynamic circuits.  By default, the classical register name is `meas`, as shown in the following example.  When defining your circuit, if you create one or more classical registers with a non-default name, use that name to get the results. You can find the classical register name by running `<circuit_name>.cregs`. For example, `qc.cregs`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"       ```python\n"
"    # Define a quantum circuit with 2 qubits\n"
"    circuit = QuantumCircuit(2)\n"
"    circuit.h(0)\n"
"    circuit.cx(0, 1)\n"
"    circuit.measure_all()\n"
"    circuit.draw()\n"
"    ```\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    ```text\n"
"            ┌───┐      ░ ┌─┐\n"
"       q_0: ┤ H ├──■───░─┤M├───\n"
"            └───┘┌─┴─┐ ░ └╥┘┌─┐\n"
"       q_1: ─────┤ X ├─░──╫─┤M├\n"
"                 └───┘ ░  ║ └╥┘\n"
"    meas: 2/══════════════╩══╩═\n"
"                          0  1\n"
"    ```\n"
msgstr ""

